<TopicPage slug="subjects/public-relations/foundational/what-public-relations-is">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Public relations is the management discipline of relationships between organisations and their
    publics — built on research, honest communication and mutual influence, not spin. The old
    caricature (press agent + smoke) has a modern answer: PRSA defines PR as a strategic process
    that earns trust, and measures it. This unit maps the profession's actual shape.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four channel families (PESO)" widget="equationStep" config={pesoCfg} />

  <h2>Why "publics", plural</h2>
  <p>
    An organisation never has one audience. Employees, investors, regulators, journalists,
    customers, neighbours — each public has its own stakes, channels and scepticism, and a message
    that reassures investors can enrage customers. Identifying and prioritising publics is PR's
    first analytical act, and it happens before any writing does.
  </p>

  <h2>Persuasion with receipts</h2>
  <p>
    The profession's ethical spine (the PRSA Code: advocacy, honesty, expertise, independence,
    loyalty, fairness) exists because PR shapes public discourse. Practically, that means
    disclosure of paid content, no phoney grassroots ("astroturfing"), and the modern measurement
    standard — outcomes and trust, not clip counts. Spin that unravels is a crisis, which makes
    honesty a strategy rather than a virtue tax.
  </p>

  <h2>PR is not advertising</h2>
  <p>
    Advertising buys guaranteed placement for controlled messages; PR earns third-party
    validation it cannot fully control. That asymmetry cuts both ways — earned coverage carries
    credibility advertising cannot, and carries risk advertising never touches. Managing that
    exchange, story by story, relationship by relationship, is the job this course teaches.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> PRSA Code of Ethics and Certificate in Principles of Public Relations content; Dietrich, <em>PESO model</em>; Grunig &amp; Hunt, <em>Managing Public Relations</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
